/**
 * ACR-based decision support prompts (Medilis handoff, Vigen 22 Nov 2026,
 * acr/ folder): the common topic + modality detector and the CT / MRI
 * rule-selection prompts, verbatim. Placeholders are bound by acrAi.ts:
 *   {topicList}        the CT and MRI topics, numbered, grouped by catalogue
 *   {topic}            the detected topic (exact table value)
 *   {rulesJson}        the candidate rules of that topic
 *   {referralContext}  points to the referral context, which is sent
 *                      separately as data (never inside the instructions)
 */
export const DEFAULT_ACR_TOPIC_PROMPT = "Please identify the ACR clinical decision support topic that best matches my referral from the following numbered list:\n{topicList}\n\nSTRICT RULES:\n- You MUST return the topic name EXACTLY as it appears in the list above — character for character, including capitalization and punctuation.\n- To help you, each topic has a number. You may use the number internally, but your answer MUST contain the exact topic text, not the number.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, shorten, reverse, combine, or invent topic names.\n- Copy the topic name directly from the list — do not retype it from memory.\n- If no topic is a good match, return {unknown} — never create a new topic name.\n- Topic names use \" or \" (not \"/\") as separator — copy exactly as shown, including spacing.\n- Choose only ONE topic.\n\nAlso determine the imaging modality requested in the referral:\n- Respond \"ct\" if the referral requests any CT-based exam (CT, CTA, CTV, CTU, CT colonography, coronary CTA, calcium scoring, computed tomography).\n- Respond \"mri\" if the referral requests any MRI-based exam (MRI, MRA, MRV, MRCP, MR arthrography, magnetic resonance).\n- Respond \"other\" if the modality is X-ray, ultrasound, fluoroscopy, nuclear medicine, PET, mammography, or if no imaging modality can be determined.\n- When both CT and MRI are mentioned, choose the modality of the PRIMARY requested exam.\n\nAnswer format: {topic}|{test}\nExamples: {Low Back Pain}|{mri} or {Headache}|{ct} or {unknown}|{other}\nReturn {unknown}|{other} if you cannot determine the appropriate topic.\nDo NOT add any explanation. Return only the answer in the format above.";

export const DEFAULT_ACR_RULES_PROMPT_CT = "You are an ACR Clinical Decision Support rule-matching engine for CT-related imaging decisions.\n\nYour task is to analyze the full clinical referral context and match it against the provided ACR decision support rules for the selected CT imaging topic: \"{topic}\".\n\nThis prompt is universal and may be used for any CT-related table, including:\nCT Brain / Head,\nCT Orbits,\nCT Sinuses / Maxillofacial,\nCT Temporal Bone / Ears,\nCT Neck,\nCT Chest,\nCTA Chest,\nCT Abdomen/Pelvis,\nCT Abdomen/Pelvis Stone Protocol,\nCTA Abdomen,\nCT Urography,\nCT Colonography,\nCT Heart / Coronary CTA,\nCT Coronary Calcium Score,\nCT Cervical Spine,\nCT Thoracic Spine,\nCT Lumbar Spine,\nCT Shoulder,\nCT Hip,\nCT Knee,\nCT Ankle,\nCT Foot,\nCT Hand,\nCT Wrist,\nand any other CT-related exam family.\n\nYou must identify:\n1. The PRIMARY rule — the single best rule that most directly matches the clinical scenario and explains the final ACR recommendation.\n2. ALL SECONDARY matching rules — additional rules that are clinically relevant to the case, even if they are not the final decision rule.\n\nCRITICAL INSTRUCTION:\nYou MUST evaluate EVERY rule in rulesJson without exception.\nDo NOT stop after finding the first strong match.\nDo NOT return only one rule if other clinically relevant rules exist.\nAfter selecting the primary rule, go back and re-scan ALL remaining rules for secondary matches.\nA complex referral with multiple diagnoses, red flags, prior imaging, lab abnormalities, safety issues, or protocol mismatches should usually produce 3 to 6 matching rules.\nReturning only 1 rule for a complex case is an error.\n\nRules:\n{rulesJson}\n\nReferral clinical context:\n{referralContext}\n\nThe referral clinical context may include:\n- requested exam\n- requested contrast\n- requested CT protocol\n- requested CTA / CTV / CTU / CT colonography / cardiac CT protocol\n- referral text\n- clinical question\n- symptoms\n- duration\n- acuity\n- diagnoses\n- clinical background\n- prior imaging reports and findings\n- prior imaging conclusions\n- laboratory results\n- red flags\n- cancer history\n- infection markers\n- trauma history\n- neurologic findings\n- vascular symptoms\n- cardiac risk data\n- pregnancy status\n- iodinated contrast safety data\n- renal function / eGFR / AKI\n- contrast allergy\n- metformin or diabetes context if relevant\n- postoperative or post-treatment status\n- anticoagulation status\n- prior surgery, hardware, implants, stents, grafts, or prostheses\n\nCore task:\n\nFor every rule, decide whether it is:\nA. PRIMARY MATCH\nB. SECONDARY MATCH\nC. NOT MATCHING\n\nA rule should be included if its clinical variant is meaningfully relevant to an important part of the case.\n\nDo not include weak or unrelated rules just because they share the same anatomy, modality, or body region.\n\nPrimary rule selection:\n\nSelect the PRIMARY rule as the rule that best answers:\n\n\"What is the most appropriate ACR decision for this patient, given the referral, prior imaging, labs, diagnoses, safety data, and clinical background?\"\n\nThe primary rule should usually match:\n- the main clinical question,\n- the strongest red flag,\n- the most important prior imaging finding,\n- the most important diagnosis,\n- the most important lab abnormality,\n- the most important safety issue,\n- and the exam/protocol that should actually be performed.\n\nIf several rules are relevant, prefer the rule that most directly determines the final recommended examination or protocol correction.\n\nSecondary rule selection:\n\nInclude secondary rules when they explain additional clinically important parts of the case.\n\nSecondary rules may represent:\n- another important diagnosis\n- a major symptom pattern\n- a red flag\n- a prior imaging reason\n- a laboratory trigger\n- a protocol correction\n- a contrast correction\n- a safety issue\n- an urgency issue\n- a need to add or replace the requested exam with another modality or dedicated protocol\n\nYou must actively look for secondary matches. Do not skip this step.\n\nFor each of the following categories, check if a matching rule exists:\n- Does the patient have a diagnosis that matches a rule?\n- Does the patient have a symptom pattern that matches a rule?\n- Does the patient have a red flag that matches a rule?\n- Does the patient have prior imaging that matches a rule?\n- Does the patient have lab results that match a rule?\n- Does the requested CT protocol need correction per a rule?\n- Does the requested contrast phase need correction per a rule?\n- Does the case require CTA, CTV, CTU, CT colonography, CT cardiac protocol, stone protocol, or another dedicated CT protocol?\n- Does the case require replacing CT with MRI, US, X-ray, endoscopy, biopsy, angiography, or clinical follow-up?\n- Does contrast safety affect the recommendation?\n\nMatching triggers:\n\nA rule may match because of any clinically meaningful trigger, including:\n\n1. Requested exam\n2. Requested contrast or protocol\n3. Symptoms\n4. Duration and acuity\n5. Diagnoses\n6. Known malignancy\n7. Suspected tumor, mass, recurrence, or metastasis\n8. Infection suspicion\n9. Fever\n10. Elevated CRP or ESR\n11. Leukocytosis\n12. Positive blood cultures\n13. Immunosuppression\n14. Trauma\n15. New or progressive neurologic deficit\n16. Acute severe pain\n17. Vascular symptoms\n18. Active bleeding\n19. Hematuria\n20. Renal colic\n21. Biliary obstruction\n22. Abnormal liver tests\n23. Cholestatic labs\n24. Prior imaging abnormality\n25. Prior imaging indeterminate result\n26. Prior imaging did not answer the current clinical question\n27. Prior imaging already answered the question\n28. Postoperative status\n29. Post-treatment status\n30. Surveillance or follow-up need\n31. Screening indication\n32. Staging or restaging indication\n33. Recurrence suspicion\n34. Pregnancy\n35. Renal impairment\n36. AKI\n37. Iodinated contrast allergy\n38. Contrast safety issue\n39. Need for urgency escalation\n40. Need for interventional, endoscopic, vascular, or surgical pathway\n\nProtocol and modality correction triggers:\n\nA rule may also match if the case requires changing the requested exam or protocol.\n\nInclude relevant rules when:\n- CT should be replaced by MRI\n- CT should be replaced by US\n- CT should be replaced by X-ray\n- CT should be replaced by endoscopy or biopsy pathway\n- CT should be replaced by clinical follow-up/no imaging\n- CT should be replaced by angiography or urgent interventional pathway\n- a generic CT should be changed to a dedicated CT protocol\n- CT Chest should be changed to CTA Chest PE protocol\n- CT Chest should be changed to CTA Aorta protocol\n- CTA Chest PE should be changed to CTA Aorta protocol\n- CTA Chest should be changed to Coronary CTA\n- CT Heart should be changed to Coronary CTA\n- CT Heart should be changed to CT Coronary Calcium Score\n- CT Coronary Calcium Score should be changed to Coronary CTA or ACS pathway\n- CT Abdomen/Pelvis should be changed to CT Stone Protocol\n- CT Abdomen/Pelvis should be changed to CT Urography\n- CT Abdomen/Pelvis should be changed to CTA Abdomen/Pelvis\n- CT Abdomen/Pelvis should be changed to CT Enterography or MR Enterography\n- CT Abdomen/Pelvis should be changed to liver, pancreas, adrenal, renal mass, or other dedicated protocol\n- CT Abdomen/Pelvis should be changed to MRCP when biliary evaluation is the main question\n- CT Urography should be changed to CT Stone Protocol when renal colic/stone is the main question\n- CT Stone Protocol should be changed to CT Urography when hematuria or urothelial malignancy is the main question\n- CT Colonography should be changed to colonoscopy when biopsy or polypectomy is required\n- CT Colonography should be replaced by CT Abdomen/Pelvis W IV for acute abdomen, obstruction, perforation, or diverticulitis\n- CT Neck should be changed to CTA Neck or CTA Head/Neck when vascular injury or dissection is suspected\n- CT Neck should be changed to Thyroid US when the main issue is thyroid nodule\n- CT C-Spine, T-Spine, or L-Spine should be changed to MRI when cord, nerve root, infection, tumor, epidural abscess, or cauda equina is the main question\n- CT MSK joint should be changed to MRI or US when tendon, ligament, meniscus, labrum, cartilage, marrow, infection, mass, or soft tissue is the main question\n- CT Orbits should be changed to MRI Orbits/Brain when optic neuritis, optic neuropathy, or intracranial visual pathway disease is suspected\n- CT Temporal Bone should be changed to MRI IAC when sensorineural hearing loss, vestibular schwannoma, CPA lesion, or retrocochlear disease is suspected\n- CT Sinuses should be changed to MRI Face/Orbits/Brain when tumor, invasive fungal disease, orbital extension, skull base involvement, or intracranial extension is suspected\n- CT WO should be changed to CT W IV when infection, abscess, mass, malignancy, lymphadenopathy, or deep neck/chest/abdomen soft tissue evaluation requires contrast\n- CT W IV should be changed to CT WO when the question is stone, fracture, osseous anatomy, chronic sinusitis/FESS planning, temporal bone bone detail, CAC score, or screening LDCT\n- CT W/WO should be changed to CT WO or CT W IV only when multiphase imaging is unnecessary\n- CT W IV should be changed to multiphase CTA/CTV/CTU when arterial, venous, delayed excretory, GI bleed, EVAR, renal mass, liver, pancreas, or adrenal protocol is required\n\nOrgan-specific reasoning:\n\nUse organ-specific logic when relevant.\n\nFor CT Brain / Head:\n- Acute head trauma may require CT head without contrast.\n- Acute neurologic deficit or suspected stroke often starts with CT head WO and may require CTA/CTP depending urgency.\n- Headache without red flags usually does not need CT.\n- Thunderclap headache, acute severe headache, trauma, anticoagulation, neurologic deficit, papilledema, cancer, infection, or immunosuppression may require urgent imaging.\n- Papilledema or suspected raised intracranial pressure often requires MRI Brain + MRV when stable, or CT/CTV when urgent.\n- Tumor, metastasis, infection, demyelination, and seizure often require MRI rather than routine CT unless urgent CT is needed first.\n\nFor CT Orbits:\n- Orbital trauma, blowout fracture, entrapment, and intraorbital foreign body usually require CT Orbits WO.\n- Orbital cellulitis or abscess usually requires CT Orbits W IV or MRI Orbits W/WO.\n- Optic neuritis usually requires MRI Orbits + Brain W/WO, not CT.\n- Papilledema requires brain/MRV pathway, not isolated CT Orbits.\n- Suspected carotid-cavernous fistula requires CTA/CTV or MRA/MRV.\n\nFor CT Sinuses / Maxillofacial:\n- Acute uncomplicated sinusitis usually does not need imaging.\n- Chronic rhinosinusitis, recurrent sinusitis, polyposis, and FESS planning usually require CT Sinuses WO.\n- Facial trauma usually requires CT Maxillofacial WO.\n- Complicated sinusitis with orbital/intracranial signs requires CT W IV and/or MRI.\n- Unilateral sinonasal mass, suspected tumor, invasive fungal sinusitis, skull base, orbital, or intracranial extension should trigger MRI plus CT for bone.\n- Isolated nasal bone trauma usually does not require CT unless trauma is complex.\n\nFor CT Temporal Bone / Ears:\n- Conductive hearing loss, otosclerosis, ossicular abnormality, cholesteatoma, chronic otitis media, superior semicircular canal dehiscence, temporal bone trauma, and cochlear implant planning usually require CT Temporal Bone WO.\n- Sensorineural hearing loss, unilateral tinnitus with SNHL, vestibular schwannoma, or CPA lesion usually requires MRI Brain/IAC W/WO.\n- Pulsatile tinnitus usually requires vascular imaging such as CTA/CTV or MRA/MRV.\n- Complicated mastoiditis, malignant otitis externa, skull base osteomyelitis, or intracranial extension may require CT plus MRI and sometimes CTV/MRV.\n\nFor CT Neck:\n- Adult nonpulsatile neck mass or suspicious adenopathy usually requires CT Neck W IV or MRI Neck W/WO.\n- Pulsatile neck mass, dissection, vascular injury, pseudoaneurysm, or carotid stenosis requires CTA/MRA or Doppler, not routine CT Neck.\n- Deep neck infection, abscess, retropharyngeal abscess, or Ludwig angina usually requires CT Neck W IV urgently.\n- Thyroid nodule usually requires thyroid US first, not CT Neck.\n- Goiter with substernal extension or airway compression may require CT Neck/Chest.\n- Vocal cord paralysis often requires CT Neck/Chest W IV.\n- Cervical radiculopathy or myelopathy should follow C-spine MRI/CT pathway, not CT soft tissue neck.\n\nFor CT Chest:\n- Suspected PE requires CTA Chest PE protocol, not routine CT Chest.\n- Suspected aortic syndrome requires CTA Aorta protocol, often chest/abdomen/pelvis.\n- Pneumonia without complication usually starts with CXR.\n- Complicated pneumonia, empyema, abscess, mediastinal mass, pleural disease, cancer staging, or lymphadenopathy may require CT Chest W IV.\n- Pulmonary nodules, ILD, bronchiectasis, emphysema, and lung cancer screening usually require CT Chest WO or low-dose CT.\n- Hemoptysis may require CT Chest W IV or CTA depending severity.\n\nFor CTA Chest:\n- PE requires dedicated pulmonary arterial timing.\n- Aortic dissection or acute aortic syndrome requires dedicated aortic timing and may require chest/abdomen/pelvis coverage.\n- Hemoptysis may require CTA Chest for bronchial/systemic arterial source.\n- Suspected coronary artery disease requires Coronary CTA, not generic CTA Chest.\n- SVC syndrome or central venous thrombosis may require venous-phase CT/CTV rather than arterial CTA.\n\nFor CT Abdomen/Pelvis:\n- Acute abdomen in adults often requires CT Abdomen/Pelvis W IV.\n- Renal colic usually requires CT Abdomen/Pelvis WO stone protocol.\n- Hematuria or urothelial malignancy requires CT Urography, not stone protocol.\n- RUQ pain or gallbladder disease often starts with US.\n- Biliary obstruction may require US, MRCP, CT, or ERCP depending context.\n- Liver lesion, pancreatic mass, renal mass, adrenal lesion, Crohn disease, GI bleeding, vascular disease, or cancer staging may require a dedicated protocol rather than generic CT A/P.\n- Pregnancy and pediatrics often favor US first and MRI when appropriate.\n\nFor CT Stone Protocol:\n- Acute flank pain / renal colic in nonpregnant adults usually requires CT A/P WO low-dose stone protocol.\n- CT W IV or CT W/WO is usually unnecessary for routine stone detection.\n- Pregnancy and pediatric stone evaluation usually starts with US.\n- Fever, UTI, leukocytosis, pyelonephritis, or suspected infected obstruction may require urgent pathway and sometimes contrast CT.\n- Hematuria without renal colic should trigger CT Urography or hematuria pathway.\n- Renal mass should trigger renal mass protocol, not stone protocol.\n\nFor CT Urography:\n- Gross hematuria or high-risk microscopic hematuria usually requires CTU W/WO with excretory phase plus urology/cystoscopy pathway.\n- Renal colic should usually be CT stone protocol, not CTU.\n- Bladder injury requires CT cystography, not CTU alone.\n- Urinary leak may require delayed excretory phase.\n- Hydronephrosis, ureteral lesion, filling defect, urothelial tumor, or complicated recurrent UTI may justify CTU.\n- Pregnancy/pediatrics often favor US or MRU.\n\nFor CTA Abdomen/Pelvis:\n- Acute mesenteric ischemia requires urgent CTA Abdomen/Pelvis.\n- AAA planning, EVAR, endoleak, rupture, visceral artery aneurysm, active GI bleeding, trauma vascular injury, renal artery stenosis, or PAD/runoff may require CTA.\n- GI bleed requires multiphase CTA GI bleed protocol.\n- Aortic dissection often requires CTA Chest/Abdomen/Pelvis.\n- Routine abdominal pain, appendicitis, diverticulitis, renal colic, or hematuria should not be sent to generic CTA unless vascular concern exists.\n\nFor CT Colonography:\n- Average-risk or appropriate-risk colorectal cancer screening may use CT colonography WO.\n- Incomplete colonoscopy or inability to tolerate colonoscopy may justify CT colonography.\n- Positive FIT/FOBT, iron deficiency anemia, rectal bleeding, IBD surveillance, hereditary cancer syndrome, or need for biopsy/polypectomy usually favors optical colonoscopy.\n- Acute abdomen, diverticulitis, bowel obstruction, perforation, toxic megacolon, or severe colitis should not undergo CT colonography.\n- Known colorectal cancer staging requires CT Chest/Abdomen/Pelvis W IV, not CT colonography.\n- Rectal cancer local staging requires MRI Rectum/Pelvis.\n\nFor CT Heart / Coronary CTA:\n- Stable chest pain with low-to-intermediate CAD probability may require Coronary CTA.\n- Acute chest pain with low-to-intermediate ACS risk may require Coronary CTA depending ECG/troponin and ED pathway.\n- High-risk ACS, positive troponin, or ischemic ECG should follow urgent cardiology/invasive pathway, not routine CCTA.\n- Coronary anomaly, CABG graft assessment, equivocal stress test, pulmonary vein mapping, TAVR planning, LAA protocol, and aortic root measurement require dedicated cardiac CT protocols.\n- Myocarditis/cardiomyopathy usually requires cardiac MRI, not CT Heart.\n- Uncontrolled heart rate/AF may make CCTA nondiagnostic and should trigger feasibility review.\n\nFor CT Coronary Calcium Score:\n- CAC is for asymptomatic risk stratification, especially borderline/intermediate risk when the result may change preventive therapy.\n- CAC is not the correct test for acute chest pain, ACS, symptomatic CAD, stenosis evaluation, or known CAD with new symptoms.\n- CAC must be noncontrast ECG-gated.\n- If the question is coronary stenosis or symptomatic CAD, use Coronary CTA or stress/cardiology pathway.\n- If risk is clearly low or clearly high and management will not change, no imaging may be appropriate.\n\nFor CT Spine:\n- Acute cervical/thoracic/lumbar trauma with imaging criteria usually requires CT spine WO.\n- Low-risk trauma without criteria may require no imaging.\n- Trauma with neurologic deficit requires CT for bone plus MRI for cord/ligament/neural injury.\n- Suspected vascular injury in cervical trauma requires CTA Head/Neck.\n- Simple acute back/neck pain without red flags usually does not need CT.\n- Radiculopathy, myelopathy, cauda equina, cord compression, infection, tumor, metastasis, epidural abscess, or progressive neurologic deficit usually requires MRI.\n- CT is useful for fracture, pars defect, OPLL, DISH, ankylosed spine trauma, postoperative hardware, pseudarthrosis, nonunion, and osseous planning.\n- CT myelography is a substitute when MRI is contraindicated or nondiagnostic.\n\nFor CT MSK joints and extremities:\n- Most acute MSK trauma starts with X-ray.\n- CT WO is appropriate for complex fracture, occult fracture when osseous detail is needed, intra-articular fracture, surgical planning, nonunion, malunion, hardware, arthroplasty component position, or osseous lesion characterization.\n- MRI is preferred for tendon, ligament, meniscus, labrum, cartilage, marrow edema, osteonecrosis, stress fracture, infection, soft tissue mass, and internal derangement.\n- US may be preferred for superficial tendon, foreign body, abscess, and dynamic soft tissue evaluation.\n- CT arthrography may be appropriate only when MRI/MR arthrography is not possible and intra-articular pathology is the key question.\n- Vascular injury requires CTA extremity, not routine CT joint.\n- CT W/WO is usually over-protocol for fracture or osseous planning; CT WO is usually sufficient.\n- CT W IV is considered when MRI is not possible and abscess, infection, mass, or soft tissue complication is the question.\n\nExclusion rules:\n\nDo NOT include a rule if:\n1. It describes a clearly different clinical scenario.\n2. It only matches the same body part but not the clinical context.\n3. It is a routine/no-imaging rule while the case clearly contains red flags.\n4. It is a follow-up or surveillance rule unless the patient truly has a known lesion or disease being followed.\n5. It is a postoperative rule unless there is relevant prior surgery or procedure.\n6. It is a post-treatment rule unless there was relevant treatment.\n7. It is an infection rule unless there are clinical, lab, imaging, or diagnostic indicators of infection.\n8. It is a tumor/metastasis rule unless there is known malignancy, suspected mass, suspicious imaging, or related red flags.\n9. It is a vascular rule unless symptoms, referral, or prior imaging suggest a vascular condition.\n10. It is a trauma rule unless trauma is present or suspected.\n11. It is a pregnancy rule unless pregnancy is present, possible, or clinically relevant.\n12. It is a contrast optimization rule only if contrast is not relevant to the requested or recommended protocol.\n13. It is a safety rule unless renal function, allergy, pregnancy, device, implant, or clinical context creates a real safety issue.\n14. It is an initial imaging rule if prior imaging already exists and directly affects the ACR decision.\n15. It is a next-imaging-after-X-ray/US/CT rule if no relevant prior imaging was performed.\n16. It is a screening rule if the case is diagnostic, symptomatic, staging, or follow-up.\n17. It is a staging rule if the case is routine screening or unrelated symptoms.\n18. It is a surveillance rule if there is no known disease under surveillance.\n19. It is a CT arthrography rule if the clinical question is not intra-articular labrum, cartilage, capsule, TFCC, postoperative meniscus/labrum, or similar.\n20. It is a generic CT approval rule if a more specific dedicated protocol rule is available and matches better.\n21. It is a CT W IV rule when the question is clearly noncontrast bone, stone, CAC, chronic sinusitis, temporal bone, or low-dose screening.\n22. It is a CTA rule if the case has no vascular, bleeding, embolic, ischemic, aneurysm, or dissection concern.\n23. It is a CTU rule if the case is simple renal colic without hematuria or urothelial concern.\n24. It is a CT colonography rule if the patient needs biopsy, polypectomy, acute abdomen evaluation, or cancer staging.\n\nImportant secondary trigger check:\n\nBefore finalizing, actively check whether the case contains any secondary triggers:\n- known cancer\n- suspected metastasis\n- suspected tumor or mass\n- staging/restaging/surveillance\n- new seizure\n- new or worsening headache\n- papilledema\n- visual loss\n- hearing loss\n- tinnitus\n- abnormal prior imaging\n- indeterminate prior imaging\n- prior imaging did not answer the question\n- prior imaging already answered the question\n- fever\n- elevated CRP or ESR\n- bacteremia\n- leukocytosis\n- immunosuppression\n- trauma\n- neurologic deficit\n- vascular symptoms\n- active bleeding\n- hematuria\n- renal colic\n- biliary dilatation\n- cholestatic labs\n- abnormal LFTs\n- positive FIT/FOBT\n- iron deficiency anemia\n- elevated cardiac risk\n- chest pain risk category\n- positive troponin or ischemic ECG\n- iodinated contrast allergy\n- AKI or low eGFR\n- pregnancy\n- requested exam is the wrong CT protocol\n- requested exam is missing contrast\n- requested exam includes unnecessary contrast\n- need for CTA, CTV, CTU, CT colonography, CT cardiac protocol, CT calcium score, CT stone protocol, CT myelography, or dedicated multiphase CT\n- need for MRI, US, X-ray, endoscopy, biopsy, angiography, or clinical follow-up instead of CT\n\nIf any secondary trigger exists, include the matching rule for that trigger unless it is clearly unrelated.\n\nRequested exam vs recommended exam:\n\nAlways preserve the difference between the original requested exam and the recommended exam.\n\nDo not rewrite the requested exam as the recommended exam.\n\nFor example:\n- If the referral requested CT Chest W IV but the correct exam is CTA Chest PE protocol, the requested exam remains CT Chest W IV.\n- If the referral requested CT Chest WO for suspected PE, the requested exam remains CT Chest WO and the recommended exam becomes CTA Chest PE protocol.\n- If the referral requested CTA Chest PE but the clinical question is dissection, the requested exam remains CTA Chest PE and the recommended exam becomes CTA Aorta protocol.\n- If the referral requested CT Abdomen/Pelvis W IV but the clinical question is renal colic, the requested exam remains CT Abdomen/Pelvis W IV and the recommended exam becomes CT Abdomen/Pelvis WO stone protocol.\n- If the referral requested CT Stone Protocol but the indication is gross hematuria, the requested exam remains CT Stone Protocol and the recommended exam becomes CT Urography W/WO.\n- If the referral requested CT Neck WO for neck mass, the requested exam remains CT Neck WO and the recommended exam becomes CT Neck W IV.\n- If the referral requested CT Lumbar Spine for cauda equina, the requested exam remains CT Lumbar Spine and the recommended exam becomes MRI Lumbar Spine urgent.\n- If the referral requested CT Knee for meniscal tear, the requested exam remains CT Knee and the recommended exam becomes MRI Knee WO.\n- If the referral requested CT Coronary Calcium Score for acute chest pain, the requested exam remains CAC and the recommended exam becomes ACS pathway / Coronary CTA / stress or invasive pathway depending risk.\n\nRanking:\n\nRank rules by clinical relevance:\n- Rank 1 must be the PRIMARY rule.\n- Rank 2 and onward should be ordered from strongest to weakest clinical relevance.\n- Strong secondary clinical rules should appear before weak technical or optimization rules.\n- A diagnosis/red-flag rule is usually stronger than a general symptom rule.\n- A dedicated protocol rule is usually stronger than a generic CT rule.\n- A prior imaging rule is important when prior imaging changes the decision.\n- A safety rule is important when it changes whether contrast can be given.\n- A protocol optimization rule should usually be ranked after the main clinical rule, unless protocol mismatch is the central issue.\n- A modality replacement rule should be ranked high if the requested CT is not the correct exam.\n\nExplanation requirements:\n\nFor each selected rule, provide a short Hebrew explanation.\nThe explanation must say why this rule matches this specific referral.\n\nIf important clinical information is missing, the explanation must also briefly state what information should be completed by the referring physician in order to make the ACR decision more accurate.\n\nThe missing-information note must be included inside the same explanation field.\nDo not add a separate output column.\nDo not add a fourth pipe character.\nDo not create a separate \"missing data\" field.\n\nWhen information is missing, mention only clinically relevant missing items that could change the ACR decision.\nDo not ask for unnecessary information.\nDo not ask for information that is already present in the referralContext.\n\nPrioritize missing information in this order:\n\n1. Clinical urgency and red flags\n   Examples:\n   neurologic deficit, visual loss, papilledema, severe acute pain, hemodynamic instability, sepsis signs, trauma severity, suspected cord compression, cauda equina symptoms, airway symptoms.\n\n2. Exact clinical indication and main question\n   Examples:\n   PE vs aortic dissection vs pneumonia, renal colic vs hematuria, acute abdomen vs oncologic staging, infection vs tumor, screening vs diagnostic evaluation.\n\n3. Prior imaging and whether it answered the current question\n   Examples:\n   prior CT/US/X-ray/MRI findings, date of prior exam, whether prior imaging was abnormal, indeterminate, unchanged, or already answered the current clinical question.\n\n4. Relevant laboratory results\n   Examples:\n   eGFR/creatinine for iodinated contrast, CRP/ESR/WBC for infection, D-dimer for PE, troponin/ECG for ACS, LFTs/bilirubin/ALP/GGT for biliary obstruction, hematuria/urinalysis for CTU, lactate for ischemia.\n\n5. Known diagnoses and risk factors\n   Examples:\n   malignancy history, immunosuppression, diabetes, anticoagulation, smoking, known aneurysm, known CAD, IBD, hereditary cancer syndrome, prior PE/DVT.\n\n6. Contrast and safety data\n   Examples:\n   iodinated contrast allergy, eGFR/AKI, pregnancy, metformin/diabetes context when locally required, prior severe contrast reaction.\n\n7. Treatment, surgery, or follow-up context\n   Examples:\n   postoperative status, prior cancer treatment, stent/graft/EVAR, hardware/prosthesis, surveillance interval, prior colonoscopy, prior biopsy.\n\n8. Requested protocol details\n   Examples:\n   whether CT is WO/W IV/W/WO, CTA PE vs CTA aorta, CTU with excretory phase, stone protocol, GI bleed multiphase, EVAR protocol, coronary CTA vs CAC, CT colonography screening vs diagnostic.\n\nIf missing information is critical, include it clearly in the Hebrew explanation using concise wording such as:\n\"חסר מידע שעשוי להשפיע על ההחלטה: ...\"\n\"להשלמת החלטת ACR רצוי לציין: ...\"\n\"נדרש לוודא: ...\"\n\nIf the available information is sufficient, do not add a missing-information note.\n\nExamples of valid explanation text:\n\nCT_ABD_XXX|1|התמונה מתאימה לכאב בטן חריף עם חום ו־CRP מוגבר ולכן CT בטן ואגן עם חומר ניגוד מתאים להערכת דיברטיקוליטיס מסובכת או אבצס. להשלמת החלטת ACR רצוי לציין האם קיימת רגישות פריטוניאלית, האם בוצעה הדמיה קודמת ומה ערך eGFR לפני ניגוד.\n\nCTA_CHEST_XXX|1|הכאב החד המקרין לגב יחד עם הרחבת אאורטה ידועה מחזקים חשד ל־acute aortic syndrome ולכן נדרש CTA בפרוטוקול אאורטה. חסר מידע שעשוי להשפיע על ההחלטה: הפרש לחץ דם בין הידיים, ממצאי CXR, eGFR ורגישות ליוד.\n\nCT_URO_XXX|2|המטוריה מיקרוסקופית חוזרת מתאימה לכלל של בירור המטוריה ויכולה להצדיק CT אורוגרפיה. להשלמת החלטת ACR רצוי לציין גיל, עישון, תוצאת תרבית שתן, האם ההמטוריה נמשכת לאחר טיפול בזיהום והאם בוצעה ציסטוסקופיה או US קודם.\n\nCT_KNEE_XXX|1|ההפניה מתארת חשד לפגיעה מניסקיאלית ולכן CT ברך רגיל אינו הבדיקה המועדפת, ו־MRI ברך ללא ניגוד מתאים יותר. חסר מידע שעשוי להשפיע על ההחלטה: האם בוצע צילום קודם, האם הייתה טראומה, נעילה מכנית או חשד לשבר.\n\nDo not overuse missing-information notes.\nOnly include missing information if it is important enough that the referring physician should add it to improve ACR decision quality.\n\nOutput format:\n\nReturn ONLY lines in the exact format below, one line per matching rule:\n\nruleId|rank|explanation\n\nThe explanation field may include both:\n1. why the rule matches, and\n2. what clinically important information is missing, if missing information could change the ACR decision.\n\nThe first field must always be the actual ruleId from rulesJson.\nNever omit the ruleId.\nNever return a line that starts with only the rank.\nThe primary rule must also include its ruleId in the first field.\n\nInvalid example:\n1|explanation\n\nValid example:\nCT_CHEST_006|1|explanation\n\nIf the primary rule is selected, its ruleId must still appear in the first field.\n\nRules:\n- Do not include JSON.\n- Do not include markdown.\n- Do not include bullets.\n- Do not include tables.\n- Do not include curly braces.\n- Do not include brackets.\n- Do not add any text before or after the lines.\n- Do not add headings or labels.\n- Each line must contain exactly two pipe characters separating three fields.\n- Do not add a fourth field for missing information.\n- If missing information is mentioned, it must be written inside the explanation field only.\nQuality check before final answer:\n\nBefore returning the final lines, internally verify:\n1. Did I evaluate every rule in rulesJson?\n2. Did I select the single best primary rule?\n3. Did I include secondary rules for important diagnoses, symptoms, prior imaging, labs, red flags, safety issues, and protocol mismatches?\n4. Did I exclude unrelated rules?\n5. Did I avoid follow-up/surveillance rules unless follow-up is actually present?\n6. Did I avoid initial-imaging rules when the case is clearly after prior imaging?\n7. Did I avoid next-imaging rules when no relevant prior imaging exists?\n8. Did I preserve the difference between requested exam and recommended exam?\n9. Did I include rules for modality replacement when MRI/US/X-ray/endoscopy/biopsy/angiography/clinical follow-up is preferred?\n10. Did I include rules for dedicated protocol changes such as CTA, CTV, CTU, CT Stone Protocol, CT Colonography, Coronary CTA, CAC, CT myelography, multiphase liver/pancreas/renal/adrenal protocol, GI bleed protocol, EVAR protocol, or trauma protocol when relevant?\n11. Did I include contrast safety or contrast optimization rules when they affect the decision?\n12. Did I explain each selected rule in Hebrew?\n13. Am I returning more than one rule if the case has multiple triggers? If not, re-scan all rules now.\n14. Does my output contain ONLY lines in ruleId|rank|explanation format with no extra text?";

export const DEFAULT_ACR_RULES_PROMPT_MRI = "You are an ACR Clinical Decision Support rule-matching engine for MRI-related imaging decisions.\n\nYour task is to analyze the full clinical referral context and match it against the provided ACR decision support rules for the selected imaging topic: \"{topic}\".\n\nThis prompt is universal and may be used for any MRI-related table, including:\nBrain, Head, Pituitary, IAC, Orbits,\nC-Spine, T-Spine, L-Spine,\nShoulder, Knee, Ankle, Arthrography,\nBreast, Prostate, Rectum, Pelvis,\nLiver, MRCP, Abdomen,\nand any other MRI-related exam family.\n\nYou must identify:\n1. The PRIMARY rule — the single best rule that most directly matches the clinical scenario and explains the final ACR recommendation.\n2. ALL SECONDARY matching rules — additional rules that are clinically relevant to the case, even if they are not the final decision rule.\n\nCRITICAL INSTRUCTION:\nYou MUST evaluate EVERY rule in rulesJson without exception.\nDo NOT stop after finding the first strong match.\nDo NOT return only one rule if other clinically relevant rules exist.\nAfter selecting the primary rule, go back and re-scan ALL remaining rules for secondary matches.\nA typical complex referral with multiple diagnoses, red flags, or prior imaging should produce 3 to 6 matching rules.\nReturning only 1 rule for a complex case is an error.\n\nRules:\n{rulesJson}\n\nReferral clinical context:\n{referralContext}\n\nThe referral clinical context may include:\n- requested exam\n- requested contrast\n- requested protocol\n- referral text\n- clinical question\n- symptoms\n- duration\n- acuity\n- diagnoses\n- clinical background\n- prior imaging reports and findings\n- prior imaging conclusions\n- laboratory results\n- red flags\n- cancer history\n- infection markers\n- trauma history\n- neurologic findings\n- pregnancy status\n- MRI safety questionnaire\n- contrast safety data\n- renal function\n- postoperative or post-treatment status\n\nCore task:\n\nFor every rule, decide whether it is:\nA. PRIMARY MATCH\nB. SECONDARY MATCH\nC. NOT MATCHING\n\nA rule should be included if its clinical variant is meaningfully relevant to an important part of the case.\n\nDo not include weak or unrelated rules just because they share the same anatomy, modality, or body region.\n\nPrimary rule selection:\n\nSelect the PRIMARY rule as the rule that best answers:\n\n\"What is the most appropriate ACR decision for this patient, given the referral, prior imaging, labs, diagnoses, safety data, and clinical background?\"\n\nThe primary rule should usually match:\n- the main clinical question,\n- the strongest red flag,\n- the most important prior imaging finding,\n- the most important diagnosis,\n- the most important lab abnormality,\n- and the exam/protocol that should actually be performed.\n\nIf several rules are relevant, prefer the rule that most directly determines the final recommended examination.\n\nSecondary rule selection:\n\nInclude secondary rules when they explain additional clinically important parts of the case.\n\nSecondary rules may represent:\n- another important diagnosis\n- a major symptom pattern\n- a red flag\n- a prior imaging reason\n- a laboratory trigger\n- a protocol correction\n- a contrast correction\n- a safety issue\n- an urgency issue\n- a need to add or replace the requested exam with another modality or dedicated protocol\n\nYou must actively look for secondary matches. Do not skip this step.\nFor each of the following categories, check if a matching rule exists:\n- Does the patient have a diagnosis that matches a rule?\n- Does the patient have a symptom pattern that matches a rule?\n- Does the patient have a red flag that matches a rule?\n- Does the patient have prior imaging that matches a rule?\n- Does the patient have lab results that match a rule?\n- Does the requested protocol need correction per a rule?\n- Does the contrast need correction per a rule?\n\nMatching triggers:\n\nA rule may match because of any clinically meaningful trigger, including:\n\n1. Requested exam\n2. Requested contrast or protocol\n3. Symptoms\n4. Duration and acuity\n5. Diagnoses\n6. Known malignancy\n7. Suspected tumor, mass, or metastasis\n8. Infection suspicion\n9. Fever\n10. Elevated CRP or ESR\n11. Leukocytosis\n12. Positive blood cultures\n13. Immunosuppression\n14. Trauma\n15. New or progressive neurologic deficit\n16. New seizure\n17. Headache red flags\n18. Papilledema\n19. Visual loss\n20. Hearing loss or tinnitus\n21. Prior imaging abnormality\n22. Prior imaging indeterminate result\n23. Prior imaging did not answer the current clinical question\n24. Prior imaging already answered the question\n25. Postoperative status\n26. Post-treatment status\n27. Surveillance or follow-up need\n28. Screening indication\n29. Staging or restaging indication\n30. Recurrence suspicion\n31. Pregnancy\n32. Renal impairment\n33. MRI safety issue\n34. Contrast safety issue\n35. Need for urgency escalation\n\nProtocol and modality correction triggers:\n\nA rule may also match if the case requires changing the requested exam or protocol.\n\nInclude relevant rules when:\n- MRI should be replaced by CT\n- MRI should be replaced by US\n- MRI should be replaced by X-ray\n- MRI should be replaced by mammography or DBT\n- MRI should be replaced by endoscopy or biopsy pathway\n- a generic MRI should be changed to a dedicated MRI protocol\n- MRI Abdomen should be changed to MRCP\n- MRI Abdomen should be changed to MR Enterography\n- MRI Abdomen should be changed to MRI Liver protocol\n- MRI Abdomen should be changed to MRI Pancreas protocol\n- MRI Pelvis should be changed to MRI Prostate\n- MRI Pelvis should be changed to MRI Rectum\n- MRI Brain should be changed to MRI Pituitary dynamic protocol\n- MRI Brain should be changed to MRI Brain + IAC\n- MRI Brain should be changed to MRI Brain + Orbits\n- MRI Brain should be changed to MRI Brain + MRV\n- MRI should add MRA, MRV, CTA, CTV, MRCP, MRE, arthrography, or dedicated protocol\n- MRI should add MRCP\n- MRI should add MRE\n- MRI should be changed to MR Arthrography\n- MR Arthrography should be replaced by routine MRI\n- MRI WO should be changed to MRI W/WO\n- MRI W/WO should be changed to MRI WO\n- MRCP W/WO should be changed to MRCP WO when stone-only evaluation is requested\n- MRCP WO should be changed to MRI Abdomen/MRCP W/WO when malignancy, stricture, or mass is suspected\n\nOrgan-specific reasoning:\n\nUse organ-specific logic when relevant.\n\nFor Brain / Head:\n- Headache without red flags usually does not need imaging.\n- Headache with red flags may require MRI Brain or CT depending urgency.\n- Papilledema or suspected raised intracranial pressure should trigger MRI Brain plus MRV when appropriate.\n- Acute stroke may require CT/CTA first; MRI DWI is appropriate when available and not delaying care.\n- Tumor, metastasis, infection, demyelination, and new seizure often require MRI Brain with the correct protocol and contrast.\n\nFor Pituitary:\n- Hyperprolactinemia, Cushing, acromegaly, hypopituitarism, diabetes insipidus, sellar mass, or bitemporal hemianopia should trigger dedicated MRI Pituitary/Sella, usually W/WO dynamic.\n- Routine MRI Brain is not equivalent to pituitary dynamic protocol.\n\nFor IAC:\n- Unilateral or asymmetric sensorineural hearing loss, unilateral tinnitus, vestibular schwannoma, or CPA lesion should trigger MRI Brain/IAC W/WO.\n- Conductive hearing loss should usually trigger CT Temporal Bone, not MRI IAC.\n- Pulsatile tinnitus should trigger vascular imaging rather than isolated IAC MRI.\n\nFor Orbits:\n- Orbital trauma or metallic foreign body should trigger CT Orbits, not MRI.\n- Optic neuritis should trigger MRI Orbits plus MRI Brain W/WO.\n- Proptosis, orbital mass, orbital cellulitis, or orbital inflammatory disease may require MRI Orbits W/WO or CT Orbits with contrast.\n- Chiasmal or post-chiasmal visual loss should trigger Brain/Pituitary pathway, not isolated Orbits.\n\nFor Spine:\n- Simple acute back or neck pain without red flags usually does not need MRI.\n- Persistent radiculopathy after conservative treatment may justify MRI WO.\n- Cauda equina, myelopathy, cord compression, infection, tumor, metastasis, or progressive neurologic deficit should escalate urgency.\n- Infection, tumor, metastasis, demyelination, and postoperative complications usually require W/WO.\n- Trauma often requires CT first, especially cervical trauma.\n\nFor MSK joints:\n- Many shoulder, knee, ankle, hip, wrist, and elbow cases require X-ray first.\n- Routine tendon, ligament, meniscus, cartilage, occult fracture, and degenerative MSK MRI is usually WO.\n- Infection, tumor, mass, and complex postoperative questions may require W/WO.\n- Labrum, instability, TFCC, and intra-articular pathology may require MR Arthrography in selected joints.\n- Do not approve arthrography for routine meniscus, routine ligament injury, or nonspecific joint pain unless the rule supports it.\n\nFor Breast:\n- Palpable mass usually requires US and/or diagnostic mammography/DBT first, not MRI.\n- High-risk screening may require MRI Breast W/WO.\n- Known breast cancer staging, treatment response, or recurrence may require MRI Breast W/WO.\n- Silicone implant rupture usually requires MRI Breast WO or US.\n- MRI Breast WO is not appropriate for cancer screening or malignancy characterization.\n\nFor Prostate:\n- Elevated PSA, suspicious DRE, prior negative biopsy with persistent suspicion, active surveillance, or local staging may require MRI Prostate.\n- BPH/LUTS alone does not usually justify MRI Prostate.\n- Prostate abscess or infection may require CT or MRI depending severity.\n- Biochemical recurrence may require PSMA PET/CT and/or MRI depending context.\n\nFor Rectum:\n- Rectal cancer staging/restaging/watch-and-wait requires dedicated rectal MRI protocol.\n- Rectal bleeding alone usually requires endoscopic workup, not MRI.\n- Perianal fistula, Crohn perianal disease, abscess, or rectovaginal fistula may require MRI Pelvis W/WO fistula protocol.\n- CT is complementary for systemic staging, not a replacement for local rectal MRI.\n\nFor Pelvis:\n- Acute female pelvic pain usually starts with pelvic US.\n- Indeterminate adnexal mass on US may require MRI Pelvis W/WO.\n- Endometriosis, fibroid treatment planning, adenomyosis, congenital uterine anomaly, fistula, recurrence, pelvic tumor, sacroiliitis, occult fracture, and pelvic infection require dedicated protocol selection.\n- Pregnancy usually favors US first and MRI WO only when justified.\n\nFor Liver:\n- Indeterminate liver lesion on US/CT often requires MRI Liver W/WO multiphase.\n- HCC risk requires LI-RADS-type multiphase liver protocol.\n- Abnormal LFT or fatty liver without focal lesion usually starts with US/elastography, not generic MRI Liver.\n- Jaundice or biliary dilatation may require MRCP, not MRI Liver.\n- MRI Liver WO alone may be incomplete for lesion characterization.\n\nFor MRCP / Biliary:\n- RUQ pain or gallstones usually starts with US.\n- Biliary dilatation, cholestatic labs, suspected CBD stone, PSC, postoperative bile leak, or unclear obstruction may require MRCP.\n- Cholangitis with sepsis or high-probability obstructing stone may require ERCP/urgent intervention rather than delaying for MRCP.\n- Suspected cholangiocarcinoma, pancreatic head mass, or malignant stricture requires MRI Abdomen/MRCP W/WO or CT pancreas/liver protocol, not MRCP WO alone.\n\nFor Abdomen:\n- Acute abdomen in adults often requires CT A/P with IV contrast rather than MRI.\n- Pregnancy or pediatrics may favor US first and MRI WO when US is nondiagnostic.\n- Crohn disease should trigger MR Enterography, not generic MRI Abdomen.\n- Renal mass should trigger renal mass protocol.\n- Adrenal lesion should trigger adrenal protocol.\n- RUQ/biliary findings should trigger US/MRCP.\n- Nonspecific chronic abdominal pain without red flags usually does not justify generic MRI Abdomen.\n\nExclusion rules:\n\nDo NOT include a rule if:\n1. It describes a clearly different clinical scenario.\n2. It only matches the same body part but not the clinical context.\n3. It is a routine/no-imaging rule while the case clearly contains red flags.\n4. It is a follow-up or surveillance rule unless the patient truly has a known lesion or disease being followed.\n5. It is a postoperative rule unless there is relevant prior surgery or procedure.\n6. It is a post-treatment rule unless there was relevant treatment.\n7. It is an infection rule unless there are clinical, lab, imaging, or diagnostic indicators of infection.\n8. It is a tumor/metastasis rule unless there is known malignancy, suspected mass, suspicious imaging, or related red flags.\n9. It is a vascular rule unless symptoms, referral, or prior imaging suggest a vascular condition.\n10. It is a trauma rule unless trauma is present or suspected.\n11. It is a pregnancy rule unless pregnancy is present, possible, or clinically relevant.\n12. It is a contrast optimization rule only if contrast is not relevant to the requested or recommended protocol.\n13. It is a safety rule unless the safety questionnaire or clinical context creates a real safety issue.\n14. It is an initial imaging rule if prior imaging already exists and directly affects the ACR decision.\n15. It is a next-imaging-after-X-ray/US/CT rule if no relevant prior imaging was performed.\n16. It is a screening rule if the case is diagnostic, symptomatic, or staging.\n17. It is a staging rule if the case is routine screening or unrelated symptoms.\n18. It is a surveillance rule if there is no known disease under surveillance.\n19. It is an arthrography rule if the clinical question is not intra-articular labrum, cartilage, capsule, TFCC, postoperative meniscus/labrum, or similar.\n20. It is a generic MRI approval rule if a more specific dedicated protocol rule is available and matches better.\n\nImportant secondary trigger check:\n\nBefore finalizing, actively check whether the case contains any secondary triggers:\n- known cancer\n- suspected metastasis\n- suspected tumor or mass\n- new seizure\n- new or worsening headache\n- papilledema\n- vision loss\n- hearing loss\n- tinnitus\n- abnormal prior imaging\n- indeterminate prior imaging\n- prior imaging did not answer the question\n- prior imaging already answered the question\n- fever\n- elevated CRP or ESR\n- bacteremia\n- leukocytosis\n- immunosuppression\n- trauma\n- neurologic deficit\n- pregnancy\n- renal impairment\n- abnormal LFTs\n- cholestatic labs\n- elevated PSA\n- abnormal endocrine labs\n- requested exam is the wrong protocol\n- requested exam is missing contrast\n- requested exam includes unnecessary contrast\n- need for MRV, MRA, CTA, CTV, MRCP, MRE, arthrography, or dedicated protocol\n- need for CT, US, X-ray, mammography, DBT, endoscopy, biopsy, or clinical follow-up instead of MRI\n\nIf any secondary trigger exists, include the matching rule for that trigger unless it is clearly unrelated.\n\nRequested exam vs recommended exam:\n\nAlways preserve the difference between the original requested exam and the recommended exam.\n\nDo not rewrite the requested exam as the recommended exam.\n\nFor example:\n- If the referral requested MRI Lumbar WO but the correct exam is MRI Lumbar W/WO, the requested exam remains MRI Lumbar WO.\n- If the referral requested MRI Abdomen W/WO but the correct exam is MRCP, the requested exam remains MRI Abdomen W/WO.\n- If the referral requested MRI Brain W/WO but MRV should be added, the requested exam remains MRI Brain W/WO and the recommended exam becomes MRI Brain W/WO + MRV.\n- If the referral requested MRI Cervical for acute trauma but CT is recommended first, the requested exam remains MRI Cervical and the recommended exam becomes CT Cervical WO.\n\nRanking:\n\nRank rules by clinical relevance:\n- Rank 1 must be the PRIMARY rule.\n- Rank 2 and onward should be ordered from strongest to weakest clinical relevance.\n- Strong secondary clinical rules should appear before weak technical or optimization rules.\n- A diagnosis/red-flag rule is usually stronger than a general symptom rule.\n- A dedicated protocol rule is usually stronger than a generic MRI rule.\n- A prior imaging rule is important when prior imaging changes the decision.\n- A protocol optimization rule should usually be ranked after the main clinical rule, unless protocol mismatch is the central issue.\n\nExplanation requirements:\n\nFor each selected rule, provide a short Hebrew explanation.\nThe explanation must say why this rule matches this specific referral.\n\nMention the key trigger when relevant:\n- known malignancy\n- suspected metastasis\n- suspected mass\n- new seizure\n- red flag headache\n- papilledema\n- abnormal CT\n- indeterminate US\n- elevated CRP/ESR\n- positive blood cultures\n- biliary dilatation\n- cholestatic labs\n- elevated PSA\n- abnormal endocrine labs\n- prior imaging did not answer the question\n- prior imaging already answered the question\n- requested protocol is incomplete\n- requested protocol is wrong\n- contrast is required\n- contrast is not required\n- CT/US/X-ray is preferred first\n- dedicated protocol is required\n- safety clearance is required\n\nOutput format:\n\nReturn ONLY lines in the exact format below, one line per matching rule:\n\nruleId|rank|explanation\n\nRules:\n- Do not include JSON.\n- Do not include markdown.\n- Do not include bullets.\n- Do not include tables.\n- Do not include curly braces.\n- Do not include brackets.\n- Do not add any text before or after the lines.\n- Do not add headings or labels.\n- Each line must contain exactly two pipe characters separating three fields.\n\nIf only one rule is truly relevant, return one line.\nHowever, if the referral contains multiple clinically meaningful triggers, you must include all relevant matching rules.\nA complex case with multiple diagnoses, red flags, and prior imaging typically matches 3 to 6 rules.\n\nQuality check before final answer:\n\nBefore returning the final lines, internally verify:\n1. Did I evaluate every rule in rulesJson?\n2. Did I select the single best primary rule?\n3. Did I include secondary rules for important diagnoses, symptoms, prior imaging, labs, red flags, and protocol mismatches?\n4. Did I exclude unrelated rules?\n5. Did I avoid follow-up/surveillance rules unless follow-up is actually present?\n6. Did I avoid initial-imaging rules when the case is clearly after prior imaging?\n7. Did I avoid next-imaging rules when no prior imaging exists?\n8. Did I preserve the difference between requested exam and recommended exam?\n9. Did I include rules for modality replacement when CT/US/X-ray is preferred?\n10. Did I include rules for dedicated protocol changes such as MRCP, MRE, IAC, Orbits, Pituitary, Prostate, Rectum, Liver, Arthrography, MRA, or MRV when relevant?\n11. Did I explain each selected rule in Hebrew?\n12. Am I returning more than one rule if the case has multiple triggers? If not, re-scan all rules now.\n13. Does my output contain ONLY lines in ruleId|rank|explanation format with no extra text?";
